WN.lb(17,{"mt_1VmTUxBrNd":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A right-angled triangle has shorter sides {=5*k} and {=12*k}. How long is the hypotenuse?","answer":"13*k","hint":"a² + b², then square root.","explain":"{=25*k*k} + {=144*k*k} = {=169*k*k}, √ = {=13*k}.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"The hypotenuse is {=17*k} cm and one side is {=8*k} cm. How long is the other side, in cm?","answer":"15*k","hint":"c² − a².","explain":"{=289*k*k} − {=64*k*k} = {=225*k*k}, √ = {=15*k} cm.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A park is 40 m by 30 m. How much shorter is the diagonal than walking round two edges?","choices":["20 m","10 m","0 m"],"answer":0,"hint":"The diagonal is √(40² + 30²).","explain":"Diagonal = 50 m, edges = 70 m, so 20 m shorter."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam says the hypotenuse of a 3-4 triangle is 7. What went wrong?","choices":["He added the lengths, not the squares","He forgot to double","Nothing, 7 is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Square first.","explain":"9 + 16 = 25 and √25 = 5. Adding the lengths gives 7, which is too long."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A TV screen is {=16*k} inches wide and {=12*k} inches tall. How long is its diagonal, in inches?","answer":"20*k","hint":"Square, add, square root.","explain":"{=256*k*k} + {=144*k*k} = {=400*k*k}, √ = {=20*k}.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"If the angle between sides 3 and 4 is 120°, the third side is…","choices":["longer than 5","exactly 5","shorter than 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Opening the angle pushes the ends apart.","explain":"A wider angle makes c longer, so c² is bigger than a² + b²."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A ramp rises {=7*k} m over a flat distance of {=24*k} m. How long is the ramp, in m?","answer":"25*k","hint":"The ramp is the hypotenuse.","explain":"{=49*k*k} + {=576*k*k} = {=625*k*k}, √ = {=25*k} m.","params":{"k":[1,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"The two shorter sides of a right-angled triangle are {k}×3 cm and {k}×4 cm. How long is the hypotenuse, in cm?","answer":"5*k","hint":"Square both, add, then take the square root.","explain":"({=3*k})² + ({=4*k})² = {=25*k*k}, and √{=25*k*k} = {=5*k} cm.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A ladder {=13*k} m long has its foot {=5*k} m from a wall. How high does it reach, in m?","answer":"12*k","hint":"Subtract the squares for a shorter side.","explain":"({=13*k})² − ({=5*k})² = {=144*k*k}, √{=144*k*k} = {=12*k} m.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which side is the hypotenuse?","choices":["The side opposite the right angle","The bottom side","The shortest side"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is always the longest side.","explain":"The hypotenuse is opposite the right angle, and it is the longest side."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A triangle has sides 6, 8 and 11. Is it right-angled?","choices":["No: 36 + 64 = 100, not 121","Yes: 6 + 8 > 11","Yes, every triangle is"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check a² + b² against c².","explain":"36 + 64 = 100, but 11² = 121, so it is not right-angled (the angle is wider than 90°)."}],"explain":{"kid":["The longest side of a right-angled triangle is the hypotenuse. It is opposite the right angle.","Build a square on each side. The two small squares together fill the big one exactly: a² + b² = c².","To find the long side, add the squares, then take the square root. To find a short side, subtract the squares, then take the square root."],"adult":["Pythagoras' theorem holds only for right-angled triangles: a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse. If the angle is wider than 90°, c² is bigger than a² + b²; if narrower, smaller.","The classic error is working with lengths instead of areas: 3 + 4 is not 5. Square, add or subtract, then square root. A quick check: the hypotenuse must be the longest side."]},"familyTip":"Measure a door or a TV: its width and height, then work out the diagonal and check it with a tape measure.","keyIdea":"In a right-angled triangle the squares on the two shorter sides add up to the square on the longest side: a² + b² = c².","visual":{"type":"squares","caption":"Square numbers are areas: 3² = 9, 4² = 16, 5² = 25. Pythagoras adds areas like these.","opts":{"n":5}},"lab":{"type":"flagpyth","caption":"Flagship lab: the park shortcut. Drag the squares on the sides into the square on the hypotenuse.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_XJJlJ6IQHx":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"The point on the unit circle at 210° is (−√3/2, −1/2). What are sin 210° and cos 210°?","choices":["sin 210° = −1/2 and cos 210° = −√3/2","sin 210° = −√3/2 and cos 210° = −1/2","sin 210° = 1/2 and cos 210° = √3/2"],"answer":0,"hint":"The first coordinate is cos θ and the second is sin θ.","explain":"x = cos 210° = −√3/2 and y = sin 210° = −1/2. Both are negative because 210° is in quadrant III."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why is cos θ negative for angles in the second quadrant?","choices":["Points there are left of the y-axis, so their x-coordinate is negative","Angles bigger than 90° always have a negative cosine","Sine and cosine swap signs every 90°"],"answer":0,"hint":"cos θ is one of the point's coordinates. Which one, and where is the point?","explain":"cos θ is the x-coordinate, and every point in quadrant II is left of the y-axis. Size alone is not the reason: cos 300° = 1/2 is positive."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"An angle of {a}° is in quadrant III. What is its reference angle, in degrees?","answer":"a-180","hint":"In quadrant III, measure past the negative x-axis, at 180°.","explain":"{a}° − 180° = {=a-180}°, so the reference angle is {=a-180}°. Sine and cosine are both negative there.","params":{"a":[181,269]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Which angle between 0° and 360° lands on the same point of the unit circle as {=a+360*k}°?","answer":"a","hint":"Take off full turns of 360° until the angle is less than 360°.","explain":"Subtract {=360*k}°, which is {k} full turns: {=a+360*k}° − {=360*k}° = {a}°.","params":{"a":[10,350],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A point on the unit circle is at (0.6, −0.8). Which statement is true?","choices":["cos θ = 0.6 and sin θ = −0.8, so θ is in quadrant IV","cos θ = −0.8 and sin θ = 0.6, so θ is in quadrant II","cos θ = 0.6 and sin θ = −0.8, so θ is in quadrant II"],"answer":0,"hint":"x is cos θ and y is sin θ. Right of the y-axis and below the x-axis is which quadrant?","explain":"x = 0.6 = cos θ and y = −0.8 = sin θ. Right and below is quadrant IV. Check: 0.6² + 0.8² = 0.36 + 0.64 = 1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"In a right triangle with hypotenuse 1, which length is the side adjacent to the acute angle θ?","choices":["cos θ","sin θ","tan θ"],"answer":0,"hint":"cos θ = adjacent ÷ hypotenuse, and the hypotenuse is 1.","explain":"Adjacent ÷ 1 = cos θ, so the adjacent side has length cos θ. That is why the unit circle's x-coordinate is cos θ."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is sin(−90°)?","choices":["−1","1","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"A negative angle turns clockwise. Where does a quarter turn clockwise from (1, 0) end?","explain":"Clockwise 90° ends at (0, −1), the same point as 270°, so sin(−90°) = −1 and cos(−90°) = 0."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which angle has the same sine as 40°?","choices":["140°","220°","320°"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sine is the height. Which other point on the circle is at the same height as 40°?","explain":"180° − 40° = 140° is the mirror image across the y-axis, at the same height. 220° and 320° are below the x-axis, so their sines are negative."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"A point on the unit circle in quadrant I has cos θ = 0.28. What is sin θ?","choices":["0.96","0.72","0.28"],"answer":0,"hint":"Every point is 1 from the centre, so cos²θ + sin²θ = 1.","explain":"sin²θ = 1 − 0.28² = 1 − 0.0784 = 0.9216, so sin θ = 0.96 (positive in quadrant I). 0.72 comes from 1 − 0.28, which forgets the squares."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"On the unit circle, what are the coordinates of the point at angle θ?","choices":["(cos θ, sin θ)","(sin θ, cos θ)","(θ, 1)"],"answer":0,"hint":"The radius is a hypotenuse of length 1. Which side is adjacent to θ: across or up?","explain":"The across distance is adjacent ÷ 1 = cos θ, and the height is opposite ÷ 1 = sin θ. So the point is (cos θ, sin θ)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An angle of {a}° is in quadrant II. What is its reference angle, in degrees?","answer":"180-a","hint":"In quadrant II, measure the gap between the radius and the negative x-axis, at 180°.","explain":"{a}° is {=180-a}° short of 180°, so the reference angle is {=180-a}°. The point is at the same height as the point at {=180-a}°.","params":{"a":[91,179]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Turning clockwise by {a}° gives the angle −{a}°. Which angle between 0° and 360° lands on the same point?","answer":"360-a","hint":"A full turn is 360°. Going back {a}° from a full turn ends in the same place.","explain":"Clockwise {a}° ends where anticlockwise 360° − {a}° = {=360-a}° does, so both angles have the same sine and cosine.","params":{"a":[1,359]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which is true for an angle in quadrant III, between 180° and 270°?","choices":["cos θ < 0 and sin θ < 0","cos θ < 0 and sin θ > 0","cos θ > 0 and sin θ < 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Quadrant III is bottom left. Is x positive or negative there? And y?","explain":"Bottom left means left of the y-axis (x < 0) and below the x-axis (y < 0), so both cos θ and sin θ are negative."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"What is cos 150°?","choices":["−√3/2","√3/2","−1/2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the reference angle, then ask whether the point is left or right of the y-axis.","explain":"The reference angle is 30° and the point is left of the y-axis, so cos 150° = −cos 30° = −√3/2 ≈ −0.866. −1/2 would be cos 120°."}],"explain":{"kid":["Draw a circle with radius 1 and its centre at (0, 0). Start a point at (1, 0) and turn it round the circle the opposite way to a clock's hands. Call the angle it turns θ.","Read the point's place. How far across it is, x, is called cos θ. How far up it is, y, is called sin θ.","On the left side of the circle, x is less than zero, so cos θ is too. Below the middle line, y is less than zero, so sin θ is too.","At 150°, the point is up and to the left. So cos 150° is negative and sin 150° is positive."],"adult":["Take the circle x² + y² = 1. Turn a radius anticlockwise from the positive x-axis through θ. The end point is defined to be (cos θ, sin θ). For an acute angle this matches SOH CAH TOA: the radius is a hypotenuse of length 1, so the adjacent side is x and the opposite side is y.","The definition works for any angle: 150°, 210°, negative angles (turned clockwise) and angles over 360°. Angles that differ by a full turn, 360° or 2π radians, land on the same point, so sine and cosine repeat.","The quadrant gives the sign. Cosine is positive in quadrants I and IV, right of the y-axis. Sine is positive in I and II, above the x-axis. The size comes from the reference angle, the acute angle to the x-axis. So cos 150° = −cos 30° = −√3/2 and sin 210° = −sin 30° = −1/2.","Every point is 1 from the centre, so cos²θ + sin²θ = 1 for all θ. Common mistakes: swapping the coordinates (cos is x, not y), using the reference angle's signs in every quadrant, turning clockwise for a positive angle, and thinking sine and cosine stop at 90°."]},"familyTip":"Hold a pencil flat on a paper plate, from the centre to the right edge. Turn it anticlockwise to about 150° and ask: is the tip left or right of centre, above or below? That gives the signs of cos 150° and sin 150° before any calculator.","keyIdea":"On the unit circle (radius 1, centre at the origin), the point reached by turning θ anticlockwise from the positive x-axis has coordinates (cos θ, sin θ). This defines sine and cosine for every angle, and the quadrant decides their signs.","visual":{"type":"geolab","caption":"Legs 8 and 6, hypotenuse 10: cos = 0.8, sin = 0.6. Scale the hypotenuse to 1 and the legs are the unit-circle point (0.8, 0.6).","opts":{"pts":[[1,1],[9,1],[9,7]],"grid":10}},"lab":{"type":"flagunit","caption":"Flagship lab: turn a point round the unit circle. Its x is cos θ, its y is sin θ, and the graph draws both as you go.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_FhJ3b20GC4":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Is a diameter a chord?","choices":["Yes: it joins two points on the circle, passing through the centre","No: chords cannot pass through the centre","No: a diameter is an arc"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check the definition of a chord. Does a diameter fit it?","explain":"A chord is any straight line joining two points on the circle. A diameter does that, through the centre, so it is the longest chord."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"One straight cut across a round cake, away from the centre, cuts off a small piece. What is that piece?","choices":["A segment","A sector","A tangent"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many straight edges does the piece have, and are they radii?","explain":"The piece is bounded by one chord (the cut) and an arc, so it is a segment. A sector would need two radii."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"The diameter of a circle is {d} cm. How long is its radius, in cm?","answer":"d/2","hint":"A diameter is made of two radii end to end through the centre.","explain":"The radius is half the diameter: {d} ÷ 2 = {=d/2} cm.","params":{"d":[4,30]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"OT is a radius and TP is part of the tangent at T. In triangle OTP, angle TOP is {a}°. What is angle OPT, in degrees?","answer":"90-a","hint":"The radius meets the tangent at 90°, so angle OTP is 90°. Then use the angle sum of a triangle.","explain":"Angle OTP is 90°. The angles add up to 180°, so angle OPT is 180 − 90 − {a} = {=90-a}°.","params":{"a":[20,70]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"The minor arc AB is 140°. Which statement is true?","choices":["The major arc AB is 220°","The major arc AB is 140°","The minor arc is the longer of the two"],"answer":0,"hint":"Minor means less than half. The two arcs make a full turn of 360°.","explain":"360° − 140° = 220°, so the major arc is 220°. The minor arc is always the shorter one."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What is the difference between a sector and a segment?","choices":["A sector has two radii as edges; a segment is cut off by a chord","A sector is cut off by a chord; a segment has two radii as edges","There is no difference"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one looks like a slice of pizza from the middle?","explain":"A sector is bounded by two radii and an arc. A segment is bounded by a chord and an arc."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A straight line cuts a circle at two points. What is the part of the line inside the circle?","choices":["A chord","A tangent","An arc"],"answer":0,"hint":"Its two ends are on the circle and it is straight.","explain":"The inside part joins two points on the circle with a straight line, so it is a chord. A tangent would meet the circle only once."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A straight line meets a circle at exactly one point and does not cross it. What is the line?","choices":["A tangent","A chord","A radius"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which line only touches the circle and stays outside it?","explain":"That is a tangent. A chord meets the circle at two points, and a radius starts at the centre."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these describes a chord?","choices":["A straight line joining two points on the circle","A curved part of the circle's edge","A line from the centre to the edge"],"answer":0,"hint":"A chord is straight, and both of its ends are on the circle.","explain":"A chord joins two points on the circle with a straight line. The curved part is an arc, and a centre-to-edge line is a radius."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A circle has radius {r} cm. How long is the longest chord you can draw in it, in cm?","answer":"2*r","hint":"The longest chord passes through the centre. What is that chord called?","explain":"The longest chord is the diameter, which is two radii: 2 × {r} = {=2*r} cm.","params":{"r":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Points A and B split a circle into two arcs. The minor arc AB is {a}°. What is the major arc AB, in degrees?","answer":"360-a","hint":"The two arcs together go all the way round the circle, which is 360°.","explain":"The two arcs add up to 360°, so the major arc is 360 − {a} = {=360-a}°.","params":{"a":[20,170]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A region of a circle is bounded by two radii and an arc. What is it called?","choices":["A sector","A segment","A chord"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a slice of pizza cut from the middle. Which name goes with two radii?","explain":"Two radii and an arc make a sector. A segment is cut off by a chord, and a chord is a line, not a region."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"How many points does a tangent share with its circle?","choices":["Exactly 1","Exactly 2","None"],"answer":0,"hint":"Picture a ruler resting against a coin. Where do they meet?","explain":"A tangent touches the circle at exactly one point. A line meeting it at 2 points cuts through it, and one meeting it at none misses."}],"explain":{"kid":["Think of a round pizza. The crust all the way round is the circumference. A line from the middle to the crust is a radius.","Draw a straight line from one point on the crust to another. That line is a chord. The longest chord goes through the middle. It is the diameter.","A bit of the curved crust is an arc. Two points split the crust into a short arc and a long arc. The short one is the minor arc. The long one is the major arc.","Cut a slice from the middle, with two straight cuts. That slice is a sector. Make one straight cut across the edge instead. The piece you cut off is a segment. A ruler that just rests against the edge, touching at one spot, is a tangent."],"adult":["Circles have named parts beyond radius and diameter. A chord is a straight line segment whose two ends lie on the circle. A diameter is a chord that passes through the centre, so it is the longest possible chord, twice the radius.","An arc is part of the circumference between two points. Any two points make two arcs: the minor arc, less than half the circle, and the major arc, more than half. Their angles at the centre add up to 360°, so a 100° minor arc goes with a 260° major arc.","A sector is the region between two radii and the arc joining their ends, shaped like a slice of pizza. A segment is the region between a chord and its arc, with no radii as edges. Each comes in a minor and a major version.","A tangent is a straight line that touches the circle at exactly one point and does not cross it. The radius drawn to that point meets the tangent at 90°. A line that crosses the circle at two points is not a tangent; the part of it inside the circle is a chord. Common mistakes: swapping sector and segment; thinking a diameter is not a chord; choosing the major arc by its position on the page instead of its size; and calling a line that cuts through the circle a tangent."]},"familyTip":"Cut a paper plate or a tortilla. Make one cut from the edge to the middle and back out to make a slice, and one straight cut across the edge. Ask which piece is the sector and which is the segment, and how they know.","keyIdea":"A chord is a straight line joining two points on a circle; the diameter is the longest chord, through the centre. An arc is part of the curved edge: the minor arc is less than half, the major arc more. A sector is a region bounded by two radii and an arc; a segment is a region cut off by a chord. A tangent touches the circle at exactly one point, at 90° to the radius there.","worked":{"title":"A and B are points on a circle with centre O, and the minor arc AB is 70°. Name the parts and find the major arc.","steps":["The straight line AB joins two points on the circle, so it is a chord. It misses O, so it is not a diameter.","The minor arc AB is the short way round: 70°. The major arc goes the long way: 360° − 70° = 290°.","The region between OA, OB and the minor arc is the minor sector. The region between chord AB and the minor arc is the minor segment.","A tangent at A would touch the circle only at A, at 90° to the radius OA."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each part a line, a region or part of the curved edge? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Straight line","Region (an area)","Curved edge"],"items":[["Chord",0],["Diameter",0],["Tangent",0],["Radius",0],["Sector",1],["Segment",1],["Minor arc",2],["Major arc",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagchord","caption":"Flagship lab: drag a chord until it becomes a diameter, pick the major arc, find the sector and the segment, and slide a line until it is a tangent.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_HXwk3pTM1W":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which expression gives the arc length of a sector with angle θ and radius r?","choices":["θ/360 × 2πr","θ/360 × πr²","θ/100 × 2πr"],"answer":0,"hint":"An arc is part of the edge, so start from the circumference.","explain":"The arc is the fraction θ/360 of the circumference 2πr. πr² gives an area, and a full turn is 360°, not 100."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Find the area of a 72° sector of radius 10 cm, to 1 decimal place.","answer":62.8,"tolerance":0.05,"hint":"72/360 = 1/5. Find 1/5 of π × 10².","explain":"1/5 × π × 100 = 20π ≈ 62.8 cm²."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"The radius of a sector doubles and the angle stays the same. What happens to its area?","choices":["It becomes 4 times as big","It doubles","It stays the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Area uses r². What is (2r)²?","explain":"(2r)² = 4r², so the area is 4 times as big. The arc only doubles, because it uses r, not r²."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Write the arc of a {=30*k}° sector of radius {r} cm as a multiple of π. Type the number in front of π.","answer":"k*r/6","hint":"Work out {=30*k}/360 of 2π × {r}.","explain":"{=30*k}/360 × 2 × {r} = {=k*r/6}, so the arc is {=k*r/6}π cm.","params":{"k":[1,11],"r":[2,12]},"where":"(k*r)%6==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ali finds the arc of a 60° sector with r = 6 cm as 60/360 × π × 6² = 18.8 cm. What went wrong?","choices":["He used the area formula; the arc is 60/360 × 2π × 6 ≈ 6.3 cm","He should divide by 100","Nothing, it is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is an arc a length or an area?","explain":"An arc is a length, so it is a fraction of 2πr: 1/6 × 37.7 ≈ 6.3 cm. 18.8 cm² is the sector's area."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A sector of radius 6 cm has an arc of 5π cm. What is its angle in degrees?","answer":150,"hint":"Divide the arc by the whole edge, 12π, then multiply by 360.","explain":"5π ÷ 12π = 5/12, and 5/12 × 360 = 150°."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is the perimeter of a sector?","choices":["The arc length plus two radii","The arc length only","The arc length plus one radius"],"answer":0,"hint":"Trace round the edge of a pizza slice. How many straight edges are there?","explain":"A sector has one curved edge, the arc, and two straight edges, both radii. Add all three."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Write the area of a {=30*k}° sector of radius {r} cm as a multiple of π. Type the number in front of π.","answer":"k*r*r/12","hint":"Work out {=30*k}/360 of π × {r}².","explain":"{=30*k}/360 × {=r*r} = {=k*r*r/12}, so the area is {=k*r*r/12}π cm².","params":{"k":[1,11],"r":[2,8]},"where":"(k*r*r)%12==0"}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A sector has angle {=30*k}° and radius {r} cm. Its arc length is how many π cm? (Type the number in front of π.)","answer":"k*r/6","hint":"Fraction of the circle: {=30*k}/360. Multiply it by 2r = {=2*r}.","explain":"{=30*k}/360 × 2π × {r} = {=k*r/6}π cm. The fraction of the circle times the whole edge 2πr.","params":{"k":[1,11],"r":[3,12]},"where":"(k*r)%3==0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A sector has angle {=30*k}° and radius {r} cm. Its area is how many π cm²? (Type the number in front of π.)","answer":"k*r*r/12","hint":"Fraction of the circle: {=30*k}/360. Multiply it by r² = {=r*r}.","explain":"{=30*k}/360 × π × {r}² = {=k*r*r/12}π cm². The fraction of the circle times the whole area πr².","params":{"k":[1,11],"r":[2,10]},"where":"(k*r*r)%3==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What fraction of the whole circle is a 72° sector?","choices":["1/5","72/100","1/72"],"answer":0,"hint":"A full turn is 360°. Simplify 72/360.","explain":"72/360 = 1/5, because 5 × 72 = 360. Dividing by 100 treats the angle like a percentage."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Find the perimeter of a 90° sector of radius 10 cm, to 1 decimal place.","answer":35.7,"tolerance":0.05,"hint":"Perimeter = arc + two radii. The arc is 1/4 of 2π × 10.","explain":"Arc = 1/4 × 2π × 10 = 15.7 cm. Add the two straight edges: 15.7 + 10 + 10 = 35.7 cm."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A sector of radius {r} cm has an arc of {m}π cm. What is its angle in degrees?","answer":"180*m/r","hint":"Fraction = arc ÷ 2πr = {m}π ÷ {=2*r}π. Then multiply by 360.","explain":"{m}π ÷ (2π × {r}) × 360 = {m} ÷ {=2*r} × 360 = {=180*m/r}°.","params":{"m":[1,9],"r":[2,12]},"where":"(180*m)%r==0 && 180*m/r<360"}],"explain":{"kid":["A sector is a slice of a circle, like a slice of pizza. Its angle θ is at the centre. A full turn is 360°.","First find the fraction. A 90° slice is 90/360, so a quarter. A 72° slice is 72/360, so one fifth.","The arc is the curved crust. It is that fraction of the whole edge, 2πr. With r = 6 cm a quarter arc is 37.7 ÷ 4 = 9.4 cm.","The area is that fraction of the whole circle, πr². With r = 6 cm a quarter slice is 113.1 ÷ 4 = 28.3 cm²."],"adult":["Arc length = θ/360 × 2πr and sector area = θ/360 × πr². Both are the same fraction of a whole-circle formula; only the whole changes, from a length to an area.","Scaling shows the difference. Doubling θ doubles both. Doubling r doubles the arc but multiplies the area by 4, because r is squared.","Working backwards uses the same fraction. An arc of 15.7 cm on r = 6 cm is 15.7 ÷ 37.7 ≈ 0.417 of the edge, so θ ≈ 0.417 × 360 = 150°. The perimeter of a sector is the arc plus two radii.","Common mistakes: dividing θ by 100 instead of 360; using πr² for an arc or 2πr for an area; using πr as the circumference; forgetting to square r; leaving out the two radii in a perimeter; and rounding the fraction early, which drifts the final answer."]},"familyTip":"Cut a paper plate into equal slices: 4, then 6, then 8. Measure the whole rim with string first. Ask how long the crust of one slice must be, then check with the string. What happens to the crust if the plate is twice as wide?","keyIdea":"A sector with angle θ is the fraction θ/360 of the whole circle. Its arc is that fraction of the circumference 2πr, and its area is that fraction of the area πr². To find the angle from an arc, turn it round: θ = arc ÷ 2πr × 360.","worked":{"title":"Find the arc length and area of a 72° sector of radius 10 cm.","steps":["Fraction of the circle: 72/360 = 1/5.","Arc length: 1/5 × 2π × 10 = 4π ≈ 12.6 cm.","Area: 1/5 × π × 10² = 20π ≈ 62.8 cm².","Check: 5 such sectors make the circle. 5 × 12.6 = 62.8 cm ≈ 2π × 10, and 5 × 62.8 = 314 cm² ≈ π × 10²."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each expression a length or an area? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["A length (cm)","An area (cm²)"],"items":[["θ/360 × 2πr",0],["2πr",0],["arc + 2r",0],["θ/360 × πr²",1],["πr²",1],["¼ × π × 6²",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagarc","caption":"Flagship lab: drag the angle and radius of a sector and watch its arc and area as a lit part of the whole circle laid flat; tile copies to see the fraction.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_qm2CQajR6R":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many radians make a full turn?","choices":["2π, about 6.28","360","π, about 3.14"],"answer":0,"hint":"The edge is 2πr. How many radii is that?","explain":"The circumference 2πr divided by r is 2π, so a full turn is 2π ≈ 6.28 rad. π rad is only half a turn."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Convert {k}π/4 radians to degrees.","answer":"45*k","hint":"π/4 rad = 180 ÷ 4 = 45°. Multiply by {k}.","explain":"{k}π/4 × 180/π = {k} × 45 = {=45*k}°.","params":{"k":[3,15]},"where":"k%2==1"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"An arc of {s} cm makes an angle of {=k/2} rad at the centre. Find the radius in cm.","answer":"2*s/k","hint":"Rearrange s = rθ: divide the arc by the angle in radians to get r.","explain":"r = s ÷ θ = {s} ÷ {=k/2} = {=2*s/k} cm.","params":{"s":[3,15],"k":[1,6]},"where":"(200*s)%k==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam uses s = rθ for a 60° arc with r = 5 cm and gets 5 × 60 = 300 cm. What went wrong?","choices":["θ must be in radians: 60° = π/3, so s = 5π/3 ≈ 5.24 cm","He should divide by 60","Nothing, it is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"s = rθ only works when θ is in radians.","explain":"60° = π/3 rad, so s = 5 × π/3 ≈ 5.24 cm. Putting degrees into s = rθ gives an arc far longer than the whole circle (31.4 cm)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Convert 1 radian to degrees, to 1 decimal place.","answer":57.3,"tolerance":0.05,"hint":"π rad = 180°, so 1 rad = 180 ÷ π.","explain":"1 rad = 180 ÷ π = 57.2957…, so about 57.3°."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Write 240° in radians.","choices":["4/3","4π/3","2π/3"],"answer":1,"hint":"240 ÷ 180 = 4/3. Do not lose the π.","explain":"240/180 = 4/3, so 240° = 4π/3 rad. 4/3 drops the π, and 2π/3 is only 120°."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Convert 2.5 radians to degrees, to the nearest degree.","answer":143,"tolerance":0.5,"hint":"Multiply by 180/π, about 57.3.","explain":"2.5 × 180/π = 143.24…, so about 143°."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Circles of radius 3 cm and 6 cm each have an arc that makes 1.2 rad at the centre. Which is true?","choices":["Both arcs are 1.2 cm long","The arcs are 3.6 cm and 7.2 cm, and the angle is the same","The bigger circle's angle is 2.4 rad"],"answer":1,"hint":"Use s = rθ for each circle. Does the angle change?","explain":"s = 3 × 1.2 = 3.6 cm and s = 6 × 1.2 = 7.2 cm. The arc doubles with the radius, but s ÷ r is 1.2 rad for both."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"How many degrees is {k}π/12 radians?","answer":"15*k","hint":"π rad = 180°, so π/12 rad = 180 ÷ 12 = 15°. Multiply by {k}.","explain":"{k}π/12 × 180/π = {k} × 15 = {=15*k}°.","params":{"k":[5,23]},"where":"k%2==1 && k%3!=0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Find the arc length when r = {r} cm and θ = {=k/2} rad.","answer":"r*k/2","hint":"θ is already in radians, so use s = rθ with no 360.","explain":"s = rθ = {r} × {=k/2} = {=r*k/2} cm.","params":{"r":[2,12],"k":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What is one radian?","choices":["The angle where the arc is as long as the radius","1/360 of a full turn","The angle where the arc is 1 cm long"],"answer":0,"hint":"A radian compares the arc with the radius of the same circle.","explain":"One radian is the angle at the centre when the arc equals the radius. 1/360 of a turn is one degree, and 1 cm depends on the circle's size."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"An arc of {s} cm lies on a circle of radius {r} cm. Find θ in radians.","answer":"s/r","hint":"θ = s ÷ r: how many radius-lengths fit along the arc?","explain":"θ = s ÷ r = {s} ÷ {r} = {=s/r} rad.","params":{"s":[3,20],"r":[2,10]},"where":"(s*100)%r==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Convert 135° to radians.","choices":["3π/4","3/4","135π"],"answer":0,"hint":"Divide by 180°, then multiply by π.","explain":"135 ÷ 180 = 3/4, and each 180° is π rad, so 135° = 3π/4 ≈ 2.36 rad. Writing 3/4 drops the π."}],"explain":{"kid":["Take a piece of string as long as the radius of a circle. Lay it round the edge. The angle it makes at the centre is one radian.","One radian is about 57°. It is a big angle, not a tiny one like 1°.","The whole edge is 2πr long, so about 6.28 strings fit round. A full turn is 2π radians. Half a turn is π radians.","With radians, arc length is easy. An angle of 3 radians on a 4 cm circle has an arc of 3 × 4 = 12 cm."],"adult":["An angle in radians is the arc length divided by the radius, θ = s/r. It is a ratio of two lengths, so it has no unit and does not depend on the size of the circle.","The circumference is 2πr, so a full turn is 2πr ÷ r = 2π rad. That gives π rad = 180°. To change degrees to radians multiply by π/180; to change back multiply by 180/π. So 135° = 135/180 × π = 3π/4.","Rearranged, s = rθ. This is the degree formula θ/360 × 2πr with the 360 and the 2π already cancelled, which is why calculus and physics use radians.","Common mistakes: dropping the π (writing 135° as 3/4 rad); using the conversion factor upside down; putting degrees into s = rθ; thinking 1 rad is about 1°; and working out r ÷ s instead of s ÷ r."]},"familyTip":"Cut a string as long as the radius of a plate. Walk it round the rim, marking each length. How many fit? Then ask: would a bigger plate fit more strings, or the same number?","keyIdea":"One radian is the angle at the centre when the arc is as long as the radius, so an angle in radians is arc ÷ radius: θ = s/r. About 6.28 radius-lengths fit round a circle, so a full turn is 2π rad and 180° = π rad. In radians the arc length is simply s = rθ.","worked":{"title":"A circle has radius 8 cm. Find the arc for 135°, then the angle that gives an arc of 20 cm.","steps":["135° = 135/180 × π = 3π/4 rad.","s = rθ = 8 × 3π/4 = 6π ≈ 18.8 cm.","For an arc of 20 cm: θ = s ÷ r = 20 ÷ 8 = 2.5 rad.","In degrees: 2.5 × 180/π ≈ 143.2°. Check: 143.2/360 × 2π × 8 ≈ 20 cm."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which turn is each angle? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Half a turn","A quarter turn","A sixth of a turn"],"items":[["π rad",0],["180°",0],["π/2 rad",1],["90°",1],["π/3 rad",2],["60°",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrad","caption":"Flagship lab: lay radius-length strings round a circle, see one radian, a full turn of 2π, convert degrees to radians and find arcs with s = rθ.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_0UJ58IxUYT":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A 4 cm side becomes 6 cm on a similar copy. What is the scale factor from the first shape to the copy?","choices":["1.5","2","0.67"],"answer":0,"hint":"Scale factor = new length ÷ old length.","explain":"6 ÷ 4 = 1.5. The difference 6 − 4 = 2 is not a scale factor, and 4 ÷ 6 goes the other way."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"DE is parallel to BC. AD = 3 cm, DB = 6 cm and DE = 4 cm. Find BC in cm.","answer":12,"hint":"AB = AD + DB. Compare AB with AD to get k.","explain":"AB = 9 cm, so k = 9 ÷ 3 = 3 and BC = 4 × 3 = 12 cm."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ali says: '4 cm became 10 cm, so add 6. The 7 cm side becomes 13 cm.' What went wrong?","choices":["He added; similar shapes multiply, so it is 7 × 2.5 = 17.5 cm","He should have taken 6 away","Nothing, it is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the copy made by adding to each side or by multiplying each side?","explain":"k = 10 ÷ 4 = 2.5, so 7 × 2.5 = 17.5 cm. Adding 6 to every side changes the shape."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"DE is parallel to BC. AB = {s} cm, BC = {c} cm and DE = {d} cm. Find AD in cm.","answer":"s*d/c","hint":"Set up AD/AB = DE/BC, so AD/{s} = {d}/{c}.","explain":"AD = {s} × {d} ÷ {c} = {=s*d/c} cm. Keep small over big on both sides of the proportion.","params":{"s":[6,15],"c":[4,12],"d":[2,10]},"where":"(s*d)%c==0 && d<c"},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"One triangle has sides 4, 6 and 8 cm. Another has sides 6, 8 and 10 cm. Are they similar?","choices":["No: 6 ÷ 4 = 1.5 but 10 ÷ 8 = 1.25","Yes: each side is 2 cm longer","Yes: they are both triangles"],"answer":0,"hint":"Divide each side of the second by the matching side of the first. Are the answers equal?","explain":"The ratios 1.5, 1.33 and 1.25 differ, so there is no single scale factor and the shapes are not similar."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A model car is similar to a real car that is 4 m long and 1.5 m tall. The model is 20 cm long. How tall is the model, in cm?","answer":7.5,"tolerance":0.05,"hint":"Use the same units: 4 m = 400 cm. Then k = 20 ÷ 400.","explain":"k = 20 ÷ 400 = 0.05, and 1.5 m = 150 cm, so the model is 150 × 0.05 = 7.5 cm tall."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A similar copy of a triangle has been flipped and turned. How do you find the side that matches the 5 cm side?","choices":["Find the side opposite the same angle","Pick the side in the same place on the page","Pick the longest side"],"answer":0,"hint":"Turning or flipping moves sides around but keeps every angle the same.","explain":"Matching sides sit opposite equal angles. Position on the page changes when a shape is turned, so it cannot be trusted."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"On the bigger of two similar shapes, a {=a*k} cm side matches a {a} cm side. Another side of the bigger shape is {=b*k} cm. How long is the matching side on the smaller shape?","answer":"b","hint":"Going from big to small, divide by k = {=a*k} ÷ {a}.","explain":"k = {k}, so the smaller side is {=b*k} ÷ {k} = {b} cm.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[3,8],"k":[2,5]},"where":"a!=b"}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Two triangles are similar. A {a} cm side matches a {=a*k} cm side. How long is the side that matches the {b} cm side?","answer":"b*k","hint":"Find the scale factor first: {=a*k} ÷ {a}. Then multiply {b} by it.","explain":"k = {=a*k} ÷ {a} = {k}, so the matching side is {b} × {k} = {=b*k} cm.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[3,9],"k":[2,4]},"where":"a!=b"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"DE is parallel to BC. AD = {p} cm, DB = {q} cm and DE = {d} cm. How long is BC?","answer":"d*(p+q)/p","hint":"AD matches the whole side AB = {p} + {q}, not DB.","explain":"AB = {=p+q} cm, so k = {=p+q} ÷ {p} and BC = {d} × {=p+q} ÷ {p} = {=d*(p+q)/p} cm.","params":{"p":[2,5],"q":[2,8],"d":[2,6]},"where":"(d*(p+q))%p==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A 6 cm side matches a 9 cm side on a similar shape. How long is the side that matches 8 cm?","choices":["12 cm","11 cm","5.3 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find k = 9 ÷ 6, then multiply 8 by it.","explain":"k = 1.5, so 8 × 1.5 = 12 cm. 11 cm adds 3 instead of multiplying, and 5.3 cm divides by k."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A photo 20 cm wide and 12 cm tall is shrunk to 5 cm wide without squashing it. How tall is it now, in cm?","answer":3,"hint":"The scale factor is new ÷ old = 5 ÷ 20. It is less than 1.","explain":"k = 5 ÷ 20 = 0.25, so the height is 12 × 0.25 = 3 cm."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Triangle ADE sits inside triangle ABC, with DE parallel to BC. Which side of ABC matches AD?","choices":["AB","DB","BC"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pull ADE out. Which side of the big triangle starts at A and runs the same way as AD?","explain":"AD is part of AB, and both start at the shared angle A. DB is only the leftover piece, so it does not match."}],"explain":{"kid":["Two shapes are similar when one is a scaled copy of the other. The angles stay the same. Every side is multiplied by the same number, the scale factor k.","Find k from one pair of matching sides. If 4 cm becomes 10 cm, k = 10 ÷ 4 = 2.5. Then a 6 cm side becomes 6 × 2.5 = 15 cm.","Do not add. Going from 4 to 10 adds 6, but adding 6 to every side would change the shape. Copies always multiply.","A copy can be turned or flipped. Match sides by the angles. Each side sits opposite an angle, and matching sides sit opposite equal angles."],"adult":["Similar figures have equal matching angles, and matching lengths share one ratio: the scale factor k = image length ÷ object length. A missing length is a known length × k. It is also the answer to a proportion such as x/6 = 10/4.","Pair the sides by angles, not by where they are drawn. In a triangle, the shortest side is opposite the smallest angle. Mark the equal angles and read the side opposite each one. This still works after a flip or a turn.","Overlapping triangles are the usual exam setting. If DE is parallel to BC, triangle ADE is similar to ABC. They share angle A, and the parallel lines make equal corresponding angles. Redraw them apart: AD pairs with the whole of AB = AD + DB, and DE pairs with BC.","Common mistakes: adding the same amount to every side instead of multiplying; pairing AD with DB rather than AB; matching sides by where they are drawn; dividing by k when going from small to large; and writing a proportion with one fraction upside down. A k below 1 still multiplies; it shrinks the shape."]},"familyTip":"Take a photo and print or show it at two sizes. Measure the width and height of each. Divide new by old for both. Are the two answers the same? Now try squashing the image in an editor: the two answers no longer match, and the picture looks wrong.","keyIdea":"Similar shapes are the same shape at different sizes, so every length is multiplied by one scale factor k = new length ÷ matching old length. Match sides by the equal angles they sit opposite, pull overlapping triangles apart first, then multiply by k or solve a proportion.","worked":{"title":"DE is parallel to BC. AD = 3 cm, DB = 5 cm and DE = 4.5 cm. Find BC.","steps":["Pull the triangles apart: ADE is similar to ABC, sharing the angle at A.","AD matches the whole of AB = 3 + 5 = 8 cm, not DB.","Scale factor from ADE to ABC: k = 8 ÷ 3.","BC = 4.5 × 8 ÷ 3 = 12 cm. Check: 3/8 = 4.5/12 = 0.375."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which triangles are similar to one with sides 4, 6 and 8 cm? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Similar to 4, 6, 8","Not similar"],"items":[["2, 3, 4",0],["8, 12, 16",0],["6, 9, 12",0],["6, 8, 10",1],["5, 7, 9",1],["4, 6, 9",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsim","caption":"Flagship lab: stretch a triangle by a scale factor, match sides by their angles, pull overlapping triangles apart, slide a parallel cut and shrink a photo.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_I1uIFFoUsb":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why is opposite ÷ hypotenuse the same in every right triangle with a 35° angle?","choices":["They all have angles 35°, 90° and 55°, so they are similar","They all have the same hypotenuse","The opposite side is always 0.574 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do the angles of these triangles have in common?","explain":"Equal angles make the triangles similar. Similar triangles multiply every side by the same factor, so ratios of sides do not change."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"sin 35° ≈ 0.574. A right triangle with a 35° angle has hypotenuse 20 cm. How long is the side opposite the 35° angle, to 1 decimal place?","answer":11.5,"tolerance":0.05,"hint":"opposite ÷ hypotenuse = 0.574, so opposite = 20 × 0.574.","explain":"20 × 0.574 = 11.48, which is 11.5 cm to 1 decimal place."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Triangle A has opposite 3 and hypotenuse 5. Triangle B has the same angle θ and hypotenuse 15. Which is true?","choices":["B has opposite 9, and both have sin θ = 0.6","B has a bigger sin θ because it is bigger","B has opposite 13"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the scale factor 15 ÷ 5, then multiply.","explain":"k = 3, so B's opposite side is 9. Then 9 ÷ 15 = 3 ÷ 5 = 0.6. Size does not change sin θ."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In triangle PQR the right angle is at R and θ is at Q. What is cos θ?","choices":["QR/PQ","PR/PQ","QR/PR"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hypotenuse: opposite the right angle. Adjacent: touches Q and is not the hypotenuse.","explain":"PQ is the hypotenuse and QR is adjacent to θ, so cos θ = QR/PQ. PR/PQ is sin θ."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A right triangle has opposite {=3*k}, adjacent {=4*k} and hypotenuse {=5*k}. What is sin θ as a decimal?","answer":"3*k/(5*k)","hint":"sin θ = opposite ÷ hypotenuse = {=3*k} ÷ {=5*k}.","explain":"{=3*k} ÷ {=5*k} = 0.6, the same as 3 ÷ 5. The {k} cancels, whatever size the triangle is.","params":{"k":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Kai says: 'sin 20° ≈ 0.342, so sin 40° ≈ 0.684.' Is he right?","choices":["No: sin 40° ≈ 0.643, because sine is not proportional to the angle","Yes: double the angle, double the sine","No: sin 40° is 0.342 as well"],"answer":0,"hint":"Draw both triangles with hypotenuse 10. Does the opposite side double?","explain":"sin 40° ≈ 0.643. The ratio grows with the angle, but more and more slowly, so doubling θ does not double sin θ."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which of these can never be bigger than 1?","choices":["sin θ, since the hypotenuse is the longest side","tan θ, since it uses the two shorter sides","Neither of them can be bigger than 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which ratio has the longest side on the bottom?","explain":"sin θ = opposite ÷ hypotenuse, and the hypotenuse is longest, so sin θ < 1. tan 60° ≈ 1.73, so tan can be bigger than 1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Two right triangles share an angle θ. The small one has adjacent {a} cm and hypotenuse {h} cm. The big one has adjacent {=a*k} cm. How long is its hypotenuse?","answer":"h*k","hint":"Scale factor = {=a*k} ÷ {a}. Multiply the hypotenuse by it.","explain":"k = {k}, so the hypotenuse is {h} × {k} = {=h*k} cm, and cos θ stays {a}/{h}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"h":[7,12],"k":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Two right triangles share an angle θ. The small one has opposite {o} cm and hypotenuse {h} cm. The big one has hypotenuse {=h*k} cm. How long is its opposite side?","answer":"o*k","hint":"The triangles are similar. The scale factor is {=h*k} ÷ {h}.","explain":"k = {k}, so the opposite side is {o} × {k} = {=o*k} cm. The ratio opposite ÷ hypotenuse stays {o}/{h}.","params":{"o":[2,6],"h":[7,12],"k":[2,4]},"where":"o<h"},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A right triangle has a 40° angle. What is its third angle?","choices":["50°","40°","140°"],"answer":0,"hint":"The three angles add up to 180°, and one of them is 90°.","explain":"180 − 90 − 40 = 50°. Every right triangle with a 40° angle has angles 40°, 50° and 90°, so they are all similar."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"In a right triangle, opposite = {o} and hypotenuse = {h}. A similar triangle is {k} times as big. What is opposite ÷ hypotenuse in the big triangle? Give a decimal.","answer":"o/h","hint":"Both sides are multiplied by {k}. What happens to the fraction?","explain":"({o} × {k}) ÷ ({h} × {k}) = {o} ÷ {h} = {=o/h}. The {k} cancels, so the ratio is unchanged.","params":{"o":[1,4],"h":[5,10],"k":[2,5]},"where":"(100*o)%h==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"In triangle PQR the right angle is at Q and θ is at P. Which side is opposite θ?","choices":["QR","PQ","PR"],"answer":0,"hint":"The opposite side is the one that does not touch the corner P.","explain":"QR does not touch P, so it is opposite θ. PR is opposite the right angle, so it is the hypotenuse, and PQ is adjacent."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"sin 30° = 0.5. What is sin 60°?","choices":["About 0.87","1","0.25"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is sin θ proportional to θ? Try it on a calculator.","explain":"sin 60° ≈ 0.866. Doubling the angle does not double the ratio, and sin θ can never be more than 1."}],"explain":{"kid":["Draw two right triangles with a 35° angle, one small and one big. Both have a third angle of 55°, because the angles add up to 180°.","Same angles means same shape. The big one is a scaled copy of the small one. Every side is multiplied by the same number.","So the fraction opposite ÷ hypotenuse is the same in both. If the big one is twice as big, the top and the bottom both double. 2.87 ÷ 5 and 5.74 ÷ 10 both give 0.574.","That number belongs to the angle, not to the triangle. We call it sin 35°. A calculator knows it for every angle."],"adult":["Two right triangles that share an acute angle θ have all three angles equal: θ, 90° and 90° − θ. So they are similar (AA). Matching sides share one scale factor k, and a ratio of two sides in one triangle does not change when both are multiplied by k.","That is why sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse and tan θ = opposite/adjacent are well defined. Each one depends on θ alone. A calculator's sin 35° ≈ 0.574 is that shared ratio, for a triangle of any size.","The names come from where θ is. The hypotenuse is opposite the right angle. The opposite side does not touch θ. The adjacent side touches θ and is not the hypotenuse. Turn the triangle and the names move with θ.","Common mistakes: thinking a bigger triangle has a bigger sine; treating sin θ as proportional to θ (sin 60° ≈ 0.866, not 2 × sin 30° = 1); calling the bottom side adjacent whichever way the triangle faces; writing a ratio upside down, which would make sin or cos bigger than 1; and reading 0.574 as a length instead of a ratio."]},"familyTip":"With a protractor, draw two right triangles that both have a 35° angle, one small and one as big as the page. Measure the side opposite the 35° angle and the longest side of each, then divide. Do both give about 0.57? Check sin 35 on a calculator.","keyIdea":"Every right triangle with the same acute angle θ has the same three angles, θ, 90° and 90° − θ, so they are all similar. Similar triangles keep their side ratios, so opposite ÷ hypotenuse, adjacent ÷ hypotenuse and opposite ÷ adjacent depend only on θ. Those fixed numbers are sin θ, cos θ and tan θ.","worked":{"title":"Show that sin 35° is the same in right triangles with hypotenuse 5 cm and 12 cm.","steps":["Both triangles have angles 35°, 90° and 55°, so they are similar.","Scale factor from the small to the big triangle: k = 12 ÷ 5 = 2.4.","The small opposite side is about 2.868 cm, so the big one is 2.868 × 2.4 ≈ 6.883 cm.","Ratios: 2.868 ÷ 5 ≈ 0.574 and 6.883 ÷ 12 ≈ 0.574. Both equal sin 35°."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which changes make sin θ change? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Changes sin θ","Leaves sin θ the same"],"items":[["Make θ bigger",0],["Make θ smaller",0],["Move θ to the other sharp corner",0],["Double every side",1],["Halve every side",1],["Flip the triangle over",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtrig","caption":"Flagship lab: resize a 35° right triangle, cut similar copies from it, name the sides of a turned triangle and swing the angle to see what sin θ depends on.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_zepH6Dsn_G":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is sin 45° in exact form?","choices":["√2/2","1/2","√2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Half a unit square has legs 1 and 1 and hypotenuse √2.","explain":"sin 45° = 1/√2 = √2/2 ≈ 0.707. 1/2 treats 45° as half of 90° in value, and √2 is hypotenuse ÷ leg."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What is tan 90°?","choices":["1","0","Undefined: cos 90° = 0, so it would divide by 0"],"answer":2,"hint":"tan = sin ÷ cos. What is cos 90°?","explain":"At 90° the adjacent side shrinks to 0, so tan 90° = 1 ÷ 0, which has no value. tan 45° is the one equal to 1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A right triangle has hypotenuse {d} cm and an angle of 60°. How long is the side next to the 60° angle (not the hypotenuse), in cm?","answer":"d/2","hint":"The side next to 60° is adjacent, so use cos 60° = 1/2.","explain":"Adjacent = {d} × cos 60° = {d} × 1/2 = {=d/2} cm.","params":{"d":[2,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which triangle shows that sin 60° = √3/2?","choices":["Half of an equilateral triangle with side 2","Half of a square with side 1","A 3, 4, 5 triangle"],"answer":0,"hint":"You need angles of 30°, 60° and 90°.","explain":"Halving an equilateral triangle of side 2 gives sides 1, √3, 2 and angles 30°, 60°, 90°. Half a square gives 45°, and a 3, 4, 5 triangle has no 60° angle."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ana says sin 60° = 2 × sin 30° = 1. What went wrong?","choices":["Sine is not proportional to the angle: sin 60° = √3/2 ≈ 0.87","She should have used 3 × sin 30°","Nothing, sin 60° is 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Doubling the angle does not double the side opposite it.","explain":"sin 60° = √3/2 ≈ 0.866, not 1. Only sin 90° = 1. The sine curve bends, so it is not a straight line through the origin."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Without a calculator, work out sin 30° + cos 60°.","answer":1,"tolerance":0.001,"hint":"Both come from the half equilateral triangle. Which side is opposite 30° and which is adjacent to 60°?","explain":"sin 30° = 1/2 and cos 60° = 1/2, the same side 1 over the hypotenuse 2. So the sum is 1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Half an equilateral triangle has hypotenuse 2 and base 1. What is its exact height?","choices":["√3","1","√5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pythagoras: height² = 2² − 1².","explain":"height² = 4 − 1 = 3, so the height is √3 ≈ 1.73. 1 comes from 2 − 1 without squaring, and √5 adds the squares instead of subtracting."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Work out (sin 60°)² without a calculator. Give your answer as a decimal.","answer":0.75,"tolerance":0.001,"hint":"sin 60° = √3/2. Square the top and the bottom.","explain":"(√3/2)² = 3/4 = 0.75. Check: cos 60° = 1/2, and 3/4 + 1/4 = 1, as sin² + cos² = 1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"What is cos 0°?","choices":["0","1","Undefined"],"answer":1,"hint":"Picture a hypotenuse of 1 lying flat on the ground. How long is the base?","explain":"At 0° the hypotenuse lies along the ground, so the base is the whole length 1 and the height is 0: cos 0° = 1 and sin 0° = 0."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"A straight ramp rises at 30° to the ground and its top is {k} m high. How long is the ramp, in metres?","answer":"2*k","hint":"The height is opposite 30°, so height = ramp × sin 30° = ramp × 1/2. Work backwards.","explain":"{k} = ramp × 1/2, so the ramp is 2 × {k} = {=2*k} m. Halving the height instead would make the ramp shorter than its own height.","params":{"k":[1,12]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A ladder {d} m long leans at 30° to the ground. How high up the wall does it reach, in metres?","answer":"d/2","hint":"The height is opposite the 30° angle, so height = {d} × sin 30°, and sin 30° = 1/2.","explain":"Height = {d} × sin 30° = {d} × 1/2 = {=d/2} m.","params":{"d":[2,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"What is the exact value of cos 30°?","choices":["√3/2","1/2","√3"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the half triangle with sides 1, √3, 2, which side touches the 30° corner but is not the hypotenuse?","explain":"The √3 side is adjacent to 30°, and the hypotenuse is 2, so cos 30° = √3/2. 1/2 is sin 30°, and √3 drops the ÷ 2."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A right triangle has a 45° angle. The side opposite it is {k} cm. How long is the side adjacent to it, in cm?","answer":"k","hint":"tan 45° = opposite ÷ adjacent = 1, so the two shorter sides are equal.","explain":"tan 45° = 1, so adjacent = opposite = {k} cm. It is half of a square.","params":{"k":[2,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which is the exact value of tan 30°?","choices":["√3","√3/3","1/2"],"answer":1,"hint":"tan = opposite ÷ adjacent. Opposite 30° is 1 and adjacent is √3.","explain":"tan 30° = 1/√3. Multiply top and bottom by √3 to get √3/3 ≈ 0.577. √3 is tan 60°, and 1/2 is sin 30°."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A right triangle has hypotenuse 10 cm and an angle of 60°. What is the exact length of the side opposite 60°?","choices":["5√3 cm","5 cm","10√3 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Opposite = hypotenuse × sin 60°, and sin 60° = √3/2.","explain":"10 × √3/2 = 5√3 ≈ 8.66 cm. 5 cm is the side next to 60° (10 × cos 60°), and 10√3 is longer than the hypotenuse."}],"explain":{"kid":["Draw a triangle with all three sides 2. Fold it in half from the top. Each half has angles of 30°, 60° and 90°.","The bottom of each half is 1. The long side is still 2. To find the height, square and take away: 2 × 2 − 1 × 1 = 3. So the height is √3.","Now read the sides. Sin of an angle is the side across from it over the long side. Across from 30° is 1, so sin 30° is 1/2. Across from 60° is √3, so sin 60° is √3/2.","Cut a square of side 1 from corner to corner. Both small angles are 45°, and the cut is √2 long. Tan 45° is 1 ÷ 1, which is 1."],"adult":["Halve an equilateral triangle of side 2. The half has hypotenuse 2 and short side 1. By Pythagoras its height is √(4 − 1) = √3. The 30° angle faces the side 1, so sin 30° = 1/2, cos 30° = √3/2 and tan 30° = 1/√3 = √3/3.","The 60° angle swaps opposite and adjacent, so sin 60° = √3/2, cos 60° = 1/2 and tan 60° = √3. Cutting a unit square on its diagonal gives legs 1 and 1 and hypotenuse √2. So sin 45° = cos 45° = 1/√2 = √2/2, and tan 45° = 1.","At the ends, a hypotenuse of 1 lies flat at 0° and stands up at 90°. So sin 0° = 0, cos 0° = 1, tan 0° = 0, sin 90° = 1 and cos 90° = 0. tan 90° is undefined, because it would mean dividing by 0. Exact values keep answers exact: a 6 m ladder at 60° reaches 6 × √3/2 = 3√3 m, not a rounded 5.196 m. A memory aid: the sines of 0°, 30°, 45°, 60° and 90° are √0/2, √1/2, √2/2, √3/2 and √4/2. The cosines are the same list in reverse.","Common mistakes: thinking sin 60° is double sin 30°; swapping sin and cos for 30° and 60°; writing tan 30° as √3, which is tan 60°; dropping the ÷ 2 so that sin 60° becomes √3; finding the height as 2 − 1 instead of √(2² − 1²); and giving tan 90° a value."]},"familyTip":"Cut a paper square in half along its diagonal and fold a paper equilateral triangle in half. Label the sides 1, 1, √2 and 1, √3, 2. Then quiz each other: which side is across from 30°? What is sin 30°?","keyIdea":"Two special triangles give exact values with no calculator. Halve an equilateral triangle of side 2 to get sides 1, √3 and 2 with angles 30°, 60° and 90°. Cut a unit square along its diagonal to get sides 1, 1 and √2 with two 45° angles. Read sin, cos and tan off the sides: sin 30° = 1/2, sin 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = √3/2, tan 45° = 1, tan 60° = √3.","worked":{"title":"A ramp is 8 m long and rises at 30° to the ground. Find its exact height and the exact distance it covers along the ground.","steps":["The ramp is the hypotenuse, 8 m. The height is opposite 30°, so height = 8 × sin 30°.","sin 30° = 1/2 from the half equilateral triangle, so height = 8 × 1/2 = 4 m.","The ground distance is adjacent to 30°, so it is 8 × cos 30° = 8 × √3/2 = 4√3 m.","Check with Pythagoras: 4² + (4√3)² = 16 + 48 = 64 = 8². 4√3 ≈ 6.93 m."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which exact value is each one? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["1/2","√2/2","√3/2"],"items":[["sin 30°",0],["cos 60°",0],["sin 45°",1],["cos 45°",1],["sin 60°",2],["cos 30°",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagexact","caption":"Flagship lab: fold an equilateral triangle and cut a square to find exact sin, cos and tan, then use them for surd lengths.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_oDlduFnemk":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"(5, 0) lies…","choices":["on the x-axis","on the y-axis","at the origin"],"answer":0,"hint":"y = 0 means no distance up.","explain":"It is 5 across and 0 up, so it is on the x-axis."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"How far is it from (2, {a}) to (2, {b})?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Same x, so subtract the y-coordinates.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a} units.","params":{"a":[0,4],"b":[5,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"In (3, 7), which number tells you how far up?","choices":["7","3","10"],"answer":0,"hint":"The second number.","explain":"y comes second: 7 up."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A square has corners (0, 0), ({s}, 0), ({s}, {s}) and (0, {s}). What is its perimeter?","answer":"4*s","hint":"Each side is {s} long.","explain":"4 × {s} = {=4*s} units.","params":{"s":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Are (2, 6) and (6, 2) the same point?","choices":["No: the order matters","Yes","Only on a big grid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Across first, then up.","explain":"(2, 6) is 2 across; (6, 2) is 6 across. They are different."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A rectangle has corners (1, 1), ({a}, 1), ({a}, {b}) and (1, {b}). What is its area?","answer":"(a-1)*(b-1)","hint":"Width = {a} − 1, height = {b} − 1.","explain":"{=a-1} × {=b-1} = {=(a-1)*(b-1)} square units.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"To plot (4, 1), you go…","choices":["4 across, then 1 up","4 up, then 1 across","1 across, then 4 up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Along the corridor, then up the stairs.","explain":"4 across, then 1 up."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which point is 4 across and 2 up?","choices":["(4, 2)","(2, 4)","(4, 4)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Across first.","explain":"Across first, then up: (4, 2)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"How far is it from ({a}, 3) to ({b}, 3)?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Same height, so subtract the x-coordinates.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a} units.","params":{"a":[0,4],"b":[5,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A point is {a} across and {b} up. What is its x-coordinate?","answer":"a","hint":"x is the across number.","explain":"The x-coordinate is {a}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9]},"where":"a!=b"},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Where is (0, 0)?","choices":["The origin, where the axes meet","The top right corner","Anywhere on the x-axis"],"answer":0,"hint":"Zero across, zero up.","explain":"(0, 0) is the origin."}],"explain":{"kid":["A grid has two number lines. The x-axis goes across. The y-axis goes up. They meet at the origin, which is (0, 0).","A point is written (x, y). Go across first, then up. Think: along the corridor, then up the stairs. (3, 5) means 3 across and 5 up."],"adult":["The ordered pair (x, y) gives the distance across, then the distance up, from the origin. Order matters: (2, 5) and (5, 2) are different points.","Points on the x-axis have y = 0; points on the y-axis have x = 0. Distances along a grid line are easy: from (1, 4) to (6, 4) is 6 − 1 = 5 units."]},"familyTip":"Play battleships on squared paper. Call out coordinates as (across, up).","keyIdea":"(x, y) means go x across along the x-axis, then y up, starting from the origin (0, 0).","visual":{"type":"coords","caption":"Move the point to (3, 5). Drag it, or use the arrow keys: across first, then up.","opts":{"max":8,"x":0,"y":0,"target":[3,5]}},"lab":{"type":"geolab","caption":"Drag each corner onto a grid point. Read its coordinates (across, then up), then take the area challenges.","opts":{"pts":[[1,1],[5,1],[5,4]],"grid":1,"area":6}}}});
